 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory**: Significantly reduced memory footprint compared to the full 256-entry lookup table. Requires storage for 23 base configurations, 24 rotations, and a 256-entry mapping table [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Time**: Lookup is $O(1)$. Triangle generation involves a permutation step, which is also $O(1)$ (constant number of vertices/edges).
+*   **Memory**: Requires storage for 23 base configurations, 24 rotations, and a 256-entry mapping table [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time**: Lookup is $O(1)$. Triangle generation involves a permutation step, which is also $O(1)$ (constant number of vertices/edges) [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths**: Simplifies the implementation of the triangle connection logic; reduces code size and potential for errors in defining 256 distinct cases [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations**: Requires careful implementation of the rotation/vertex permutation logic. Does not inherently solve topological ambiguities [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Symmetry Mapping was introduced as a method to simplify the Marching Cubes algorithm by reducing the number of unique cases that need to be handled [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the rotational symmetries of the cube to map all 256 configurations to a smaller set of canonical forms [S1, unofficial]. The specific implementation details provided come from an unofficial source [S1, unofficial].
